\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Opposite choices on a spatial momentum axis}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Take distinct axes #{t,c,a,b}, so #{n\ge4}. Let the
non-self-opposite label #{r} be supported on the spatial axis
#{c}. Choose equal periods on the spatial transverse axes
#{a,b}. For free periodic spin fields, take a supplied graded
carrier and the maps of \ref{fdip-0176}, with its transverse
condition on the time map. The lifted turn fixes both labels,
as in \ref{fdip-016Y}. Choose #{a_r=u_{ab}} at #{r} and
#{a_{-r}=u_{ab}^{-1}} at #{-r}, using the same primitive
eighth root.}

\p{Here #{hr=-r} and #{\theta_s r=r}. Thus #{\mathcal P},
#{\mathcal Q} and #{S_t} all take #{r} to #{-r}. Each
conjugates the chosen source generator to the inverse of the
chosen target generator. In \ref{fdip-0175} this gives
#{d=-1}. The new degree is #{-\nu} for #{\mathcal P} and
#{\nu} for #{\mathcal Q} and #{S_t}. This comparison concerns
the specified cyclic subgroups. For a connection to admit
ordinary translation blocks for its operator, the translation
criterion is \ref{fdip-016L}.}
